.TH NINE\-MANAGE\-ANUBIS 1 "2026" "nine-manage-anubis 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nine-manage-anubis \- manage Anubis bot protection on nine.ch Managed Servers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nine-manage-anubis
.RI [ global\-options ]
.I command
.RI [ command\-options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nine-manage-anubis
automates the full
.BR Anubis
(https://github.com/TecharoHQ/anubis) lifecycle on
.B nine.ch
Managed Servers: installation, enabling and disabling protection per
virtual host (vhost), batch operations, upgrades with rolling restart,
status reporting, and a self-test.
.PP
Anubis sits between two Apache vhosts. A public vhost on port 443 proxies
to a per\-instance Anubis process listening on a loopback port in the
7010\-7999 range, which in turn proxies to a private
.BR origin\-\fIdomain\fR
vhost serving the same webroot. Enabling a domain creates the instance,
installs origin fixups in the webroot, and cuts the public vhost over to
the proxy template.
.PP
Global options must appear
.B before
the subcommand; command options appear
.B after
it:
.PP
.RS 4
nine-manage-anubis \-\-dry-run enable example.com \-\-prepare-only
.RE
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Show what would be done without making any changes. No files are written,
no services are restarted, no vhosts are changed. Safe to run against
production; use it before every batch operation.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit results as JSON instead of human\-readable text. See
.B OUTPUT FORMATS
below.
.TP
.BI \-\-anubis\-user " USER"
The system user that runs the Anubis binary and owns its config and key
files. Defaults to the
.B anubis_user
field of the config file, or
.BR www\-anubis .
This is
.B not
the website user that owns the webroot.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS install
Set up Anubis infrastructure on the server: create the system user,
download the binary, and install the systemd template. Does not protect
any domains; use
.B enable
for that.
.PP
.RS 4
nine-manage-anubis install
.RI [ \-\-version " VERSION" ]
.RI [ \-\-init\-policy ]
.RE
.TP
.BI \-\-version " VERSION"
Anubis version to download. Must be a three\-part version without a leading
.BR v ,
matching a release tag from
.IR https://github.com/TecharoHQ/anubis/releases .
Defaults to the
.B anubis_version
config field, or
.BR 1.27.0 .
.TP
.B \-\-init\-policy
Extract the embedded default bot policy from the binary to the path
configured in
.BR policy_file .
Ignored with a warning when
.B policy_file
is not set.
.PP
The command is idempotent: each step checks for existence before acting.
.SS uninstall
Remove Anubis infrastructure: the systemd template, the binary, and the
system user. Refuses to run while any Anubis instance still exists,
listing the exact
.B disable
commands to run first.
.PP
.RS 4
nine-manage-anubis uninstall
.RE
.SS enable
Put one or more vhosts behind Anubis.
.PP
.RS 4
nine-manage-anubis enable
.I domain
.RI [ domain " ...]"
.RI [ options ]
.br
nine-manage-anubis enable
.B \-\-all \-\-user
.I user
.RI [ options ]
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-prepare\-only
Do everything except the cutover: generate the key, write the env file,
install fixups, create the origin vhost, and start the service. The public
vhost keeps serving directly, so there is no traffic impact.
.TP
.B \-\-cutover\-only
Only perform the cutover: switch the public vhost to the proxy template.
Assumes
.B \-\-prepare\-only
was run previously.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Enable every vhost owned by
.B \-\-user
that is not yet behind Anubis. Requires
.BR \-\-user .
.TP
.BI \-\-user " USER"
Website user to filter
.B \-\-all
by (for example
.BR www\-example ).
.TP
.BI \-\-skip " PATTERN"
Skip domains matching a shell glob pattern. Repeatable. Example:
.BR "\-\-skip 'vorlage*' \-\-skip '*.test'" .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-notify\-services
Skip the Apache reload after each vhost change; reload once at the end of
the batch instead.
.PP
The full flow for each domain is: validate the vhost; allocate (or reuse)
a port pair; generate a JWT signing key; write the env file; install the
systemd template if needed; install the origin fixups; create the
.BR origin\-\fIdomain\fR
vhost; start the service; create a Let's Encrypt certificate if needed;
and cut over the public vhost. When a domain shares a webroot with an
already\-protected vhost, the existing instance is reused and no new
instance is created (multisite reuse).
.PP
If a step fails after changes have begun, all changes are rolled back in
reverse order. See
.B ROLLBACK
below.
.SS disable
Remove Anubis protection from one or more vhosts.
.PP
.RS 4
nine-manage-anubis disable
.I domain
.RI [ domain " ...]"
.RI [ options ]
.br
nine-manage-anubis disable
.B \-\-all \-\-user
.I user
.RI [ options ]
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-all
Disable every Anubis\-protected vhost owned by
.BR \-\-user .
Requires
.BR \-\-user .
.TP
.BI \-\-user " USER"
Website user to filter
.B \-\-all
by.
.TP
.BI \-\-skip " PATTERN"
Skip domains matching a shell glob pattern. Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-notify\-services
Skip the Apache reload after each vhost change; reload once at the end of
the batch instead.
.PP
The public vhost is switched back to its direct template. If it was the
last vhost on the instance's port the instance is torn down (stop the
service, remove the origin vhost, restore the fixup files, remove the env
file and key). If other vhosts still share the port the instance is left
running and nothing else is touched. A teardown that fails part\-way is
rolled back.
.SS upgrade
Download a new Anubis binary and restart all instances. By default,
restarts one instance at a time with a health check after each, stopping
on the first failure.
.PP
.RS 4
nine-manage-anubis upgrade
.RI [ \-\-version " VERSION" ]
.RI [ \-\-no\-rolling ]
.RE
.TP
.BI \-\-version " VERSION"
Target version to download. Defaults to the latest release resolved from
the GitHub API.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-rolling
Restart all instances at once instead of one by one. No health checks
between restarts.
.PP
A rolling restart checks that the service is
.B active
and probes
.B http://localhost:\fIport\fB/\fR
with a routable client address (RFC 5737
.BR 203.0.113.7 )
in both the
.B X\-Real\-Ip
and
.B X\-Forwarded\-For
headers, expecting a 2xx or 3xx response. Any failure stops the roll;
instances already restarted are on the new binary, the rest remain on the
old one.
.SS restart
Restart all Anubis instances without downloading a new binary. Use after
editing the shared policy file or any env file.
.PP
.RS 4
nine-manage-anubis restart
.RI [ \-\-no\-rolling ]
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-no\-rolling
Restart all instances at once instead of one by one.
.PP
The health\-check behavior is identical to
.BR upgrade .
.SS status
List Anubis instances on the host. Instances are discovered by combining
vhost configuration (assigned ports), env files (claimed ports), and
systemd (running services).
.PP
.RS 4
nine-manage-anubis status
.RI [ \-\-domain " DOMAIN" ]
.RI [ \-\-health ]
.RE
.TP
.BI \-\-domain " DOMAIN"
Filter by domain. Matches the instance's primary domain or any vhost
proxying to its port.
.TP
.B \-\-health
Actively curl each running instance and report its HTTP status. Inactive
instances report
.BR inactive .
.PP
The table has the columns
.BR DOMAIN ,
.BR PORT ,
.BR METRICS ,
.BR USER ,
.BR STATE ,
.BR VERSION ,
and
.BR VHOSTS ,
plus
.B HEALTH
when
.B \-\-health
is given. The
.B DOMAIN
column names the instance (the first domain configured for the webroot);
the
.B VHOSTS
column lists every domain protected by it.
.SS self-test
Verify infrastructure health: the user exists, the binary runs, the
systemd template is installed, and every instance is active and
HTTP\-responding. Any HTTP status code counts as a pass.
.PP
.RS 4
nine-manage-anubis self-test
.RE
.PP
Failures are reported as warnings with an error of
.IR "\fIN\fB check(s) failed\fR" ;
the process still exits 0, so inspect stderr or the JSON
.B error
field.
.SS config
Show current settings and the config file location, or create a starter
config file.
.PP
.RS 4
nine-manage-anubis config
.RI [ \-\-init ]
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-init
Create a default config file at
.IR ~/.config/nine\-manage\-anubis/config.json ,
overwriting any existing file.
.PP
Without
.BR \-\-init ,
prints the config file path, whether it exists, and the effective values
of
.BR anubis_user ,
.BR anubis_version ,
and
.BR policy_file .
The
.B config
command remains reachable even when the config file is rejected or
ignored, so a bad file cannot lock you out of the tool that repairs it.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
Defaults are read from
.IR ~/.config/nine\-manage\-anubis/config.json .
All fields are optional; a missing field or one set to
.B null
uses its default. Command\-line flags override file values.
.PP
.nf
{
    "anubis_user": "www-anubis",
    "anubis_version": "1.27.0",
    "policy_file": null
}
.fi
.TP
.B anubis_user
System user that runs the Anubis binary. Default
.BR www\-anubis .
.TP
.B anubis_version
Default version for
.B install
and
.BR upgrade .
Default
.BR 1.27.0 .
.TP
.B policy_file
Absolute path to a shared bot policy. When set, every instance gets
.B POLICY_FNAME=\fIpath\fR
in its env file, so editing one file and running
.B restart
updates all instances. When
.BR null ,
instances use the embedded default policy.
.PP
A file that cannot be read or parsed is reported and the run continues on
defaults. A file that parses but carries a malformed value is rejected and
the run stops, because that value was about to be spliced into a
.B sudo
command.
.SH INPUT VALIDATION
Every domain, system user, version, and path reaches a
.B sudo
command, so each is first matched against a whitelist. A value that does
not match is rejected with a message naming the value and the expected
form, and the tool exits 1 before any command is built.
.TP
.B Domain
Lowercase letters, digits, dots, and hyphens, as dot\-separated DNS
labels; at most 253 characters.
.TP
.B System user
.BR [a\-z_][a\-z0\-9_\-]* ,
at most 32 characters.
.TP
.B Version
Three\-part version, no leading
.BR v .
.TP
.B Path
Absolute; letters, digits,
.BR . _ \- /
only; no
.B ..
segment.
.PP
The same whitelist is applied to values read back from vhost JSON, env
files, webroot files, the config file, and the GitHub releases API. A
malformed value from any of those is treated as tampering and aborts the
run; a well\-formed value that simply is not Anubis's is skipped.
.SH OUTPUT FORMATS
With
.BR \-\-json ,
.B status
emits an array of objects with the keys
.BR domain ,
.BR port ,
.BR metrics_port ,
.BR user ,
.BR state ,
.BR version ,
.BR vhosts ,
.BR vhost_count ,
and (with
.BR \-\-health )
.BR health .
.PP
Other commands emit a single object:
.PP
.nf
{
  "steps":    ["Step 1", "Step 2"],
  "warnings": ["optional warning"],
  "error":    "optional error message"
}
.fi
.PP
The
.B warnings
and
.B error
keys are omitted when empty. In human\-readable output, steps are printed
as a numbered list under a title, warnings and errors go to standard error.
With
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
the title is prefixed with
.BR "[DRY RUN]" .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, or
.B self-test
completed (even with reported failures).
.TP
.B 1
Input rejected by validation, a command failed, no domains were found to
process, or at least one domain in a batch failed.
.TP
.B 2
Argument parsing failed.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted (Ctrl\-C).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/nine\-manage\-anubis/config.json
Configuration file.
.TP
.I ~/.config/anubis/\fIdomain\fR.env
Per\-instance environment file (mode 0600).
.TP
.I ~/.config/anubis/\fIdomain\fR.key
Per\-instance JWT signing key (mode 0600).
.TP
.I ~/.config/systemd/user/anubis@.service
Systemd user\-service template.
.TP
.I ~/bin/anubis
The Anubis binary.
.TP
.I /run/lock/nine\-manage\-anubis\-ports.lock
Exclusive lock held while a port pair is allocated.
.SH PORT ALLOCATION
Instances use the 7010\-7999 range, two ports each: an app port
.RB ( BIND )
and a metrics port
.RB ( METRICS_BIND ),
allocated in stride\-two pairs (7010/7011, 7012/7013, ...). A port is free
only if it is absent from listening sockets, from the
.B BIND
lines of every env file, and from the
.B PROXYPORT
of every proxy vhost. Allocation runs under an exclusive
.BR flock (2)
on the lock file above, held from the first read until the env file records
the pair.
.SH ROLLBACK
.B enable
and
.B disable
either complete or restore the host. Each step registers its undo, and a
failure runs the undos in reverse order. The report names each artifact
that went back. An undo that itself fails becomes a warning naming what
needs manual cleanup and does not stop the remaining undos.
.SH EXAMPLES
Enable a single domain:
.PP
.RS 4
nine-manage-anubis enable example.com
.RE
.PP
Preview a batch, then run it zero\-downtime:
.PP
.RS 4
nine-manage-anubis \-\-dry\-run enable \-\-all \-\-user www\-example
.br
nine-manage-anubis enable \-\-all \-\-user www\-example \-\-prepare\-only \-\-no\-notify\-services
.br
\fI# wait ~5 minutes for PHP\-FPM to pick up the .user.ini shim\fR
.br
nine-manage-anubis enable \-\-all \-\-user www\-example \-\-cutover\-only \-\-no\-notify\-services \-\-skip "vorlage*"
.RE
.PP
Check a single domain's status, with a live probe:
.PP
.RS 4
nine-manage-anubis status \-\-domain example.com \-\-health
.RE
.PP
Monitor from a script:
.PP
.RS 4
nine-manage-anubis \-\-json status \-\-health | jq '.[] | select(.state != "active") | .domain'
.RE
